    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Aristocracy, Greek for “rule of the best”, was praised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> as government by the most virtuous, but in practice it almost always meant rule by a hereditary, landowning nobility.</li><li>Noble elites governed most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> for over two thousand years, through bodies as varied as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>’s Great Council, the Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s House of Lords.</li><li>Revolutions, new commercial and industrial wealth, mass democracy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> stripped aristocracies of their power; only remnants, such as the noble seats in Tonga’s parliament, survive.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>From 1652 a single deputy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> could end a session and void all it had passed by declaring “I do not allow”; this noble privilege, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberum-veto">liberum veto</a>, was abolished only in 1791.</p></aside>
    </section>

